<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hippeis">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippeis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as hippeus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hippeis (singular hippeus) is the Greek term for cavalry, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> after the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> the name of the second highest of the four social classes: men with a yearly income of at least 300 medimnoi, enough to buy and keep a war horse. The Athenian cavalry, formed after the Greco-Persian War in the 5th century BCE, grew from 300 men to 1,200 and was commanded by two hipparchs, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boule-ancient-greece">boule</a> examined its members and their equipment. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> the hippeis were the royal guard of honour, 300 youths under thirty who served as heavy infantry in the king's bodyguard. Their counterparts were the Roman equites and medieval knights.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippeis.jpg" alt="Hippeis" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rider Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rider_BM_B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Greek cavalry developed in three stages, beginning in the Mycenaean period around 1400 BCE. Horses were first trained for war to pull <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, which carried infantry to battle and took the wounded away. Later the horse itself replaced the chariot as the fighting platform, and riders screened the army, harassed the enemy and pursued those who fled. The cavalry charge appeared only in the 4th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Under the Timocratic Constitution the average Athenian citizen earned less than 200 medimnoi a year, well below the income of the hippeis. The Athenian force of 1,200 included 200 mounted bowmen, replaced between 395 and 360 BCE by the javelin-armed prodromoi, and 1,000 citizens. Under the two hipparchs served five phylarchi, each commanding a tribal unit, all drawn from the two highest classes.</p><p>The popular assembly decided how many horsemen to send out. Each rider received equipment money on joining and a subsidy for a groom and two horses, which grew into an annual state grant of forty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a>, though regular pay was given only in the field. The cavalry kept drilling in peacetime and rode in the processions of public festivals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> formed a regular body of horse only in 424 BCE. The rich provided the horses, equipment and armour, while men judged unfit to serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> were drafted as riders in wartime without any drill; later each hoplite mora seems to have had 60 horsemen. Greek citizen cavalry was of limited use because of its heavy armour of helmet, mail and cuisses. Riders carried a two-edged sword and a spear but no shield, and had neither stirrups nor horseshoes.</p><p>Cavalry first became important in the Macedonian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, in whose later army horsemen made up nearly a sixth of the force. It was divided into heavy and light squadrons, or ilai, of about 200 men, the light prodromoi including sarissophoroi armed with the long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hippeis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>hippeis</b>, or knights, were the second class: men whose land yielded at least 300 medimnoi a year, enough, in Aristotle’s account, to keep a horse.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
